Revision history for SICK

{{$NEXT}}
  - Initial release. SICK brings certificates of an internal CA (step-ca) to
    devices that cannot fetch them themselves and renews them before they
    expire. A device list in YAML is the whole configuration. X.509 goes
    through the openssl command line, every external program is started
    without a shell. Runtime dependencies beyond core Perl: Moo,
    namespace::autoclean, Type::Tiny, Module::Runtime, Path::Tiny,
    JSON::MaybeXS and YAML::PP.
  - The command line sick: check (what does every device present; changes
    nothing), run (--force, --dry-run, --verbose), inventory and trust.
    Exit code 0 when everything is in order, 1 when a device failed, was
    deferred or could not be checked, 2 for a wrong command line or device
    list, 3 when another sick run holds the lock.
  - The device list has the sections ca, defaults and devices; unknown keys
    are an error with their path. A device has type, names, host, port,
    lifetime, renew_before, key, an optional owner and the options of its
    type. Secrets are read from files and never appear in a message.
  - The decision per device: ok, missing, foreign-ca, names or expiring. A
    deployment only counts when the device presents the certificate that was
    issued; a device that fails or does not answer does not stop the others,
    and an unreachable device is never renewed. A new certificate whose
    private key does not belong to it is not deployed.
  - sick run keeps one record per device in the state directory (key
    "state", default /var/lib/sick): the last look, the last certificate
    issued, the last deployment, failures in a row and the back-off; never
    a key. The run takes a lock there. A device whose attempt failed after
    the decision to renew is deferred for 1, 2, 4 ... at most 24 hours, less
    a 15-minute grace; --force tries anyway.
  - sick check --format json|prometheus reports per device the live
    certificate, renew_before, the earliest end along its chain to the root
    and the figures from the records, and for the CA its certificates and
    whether it answers; --output FILE replaces a file at once, for the
    textfile collector of node_exporter. sick inventory [--format
    table|json] [--live] lists what was issued, deployed and last seen.
  - Issuers: step-ca (the step CLI against a JWK provisioner) and selfsigned
    (plain openssl, for tests and for trying things out; created under a
    lock, a half CA is an error). The ca section takes root, intermediates,
    additional_roots (the next and the previous root during a root
    replacement) and name. ca.root has to be a single self-signed CA
    certificate and every intermediate a CA certificate leading to it;
    otherwise each device fails with a message naming key, file and reason.
  - Device types: files (cert.pem, chain.pem, fullchain.pem and key.pem as
    links into one generation, switched in one step; a failed reload goes
    back to the previous generation) and acmesh (hands the certificate to a
    deploy hook of acme.sh; secrets reach the hook through its environment
    only). A device type is a class under SICK::Target::, "cisco-sb" loads
    SICK::Target::CiscoSB; it gets the roots of the CA as trust_roots, can
    name a default_port and verifies a device before it sends it a secret.
    Further device types are their own distributions: SICK-Target-CiscoSB
    (cisco-sb) and SICK-Target-Proxmox (proxmox-ve, proxmox-bs).
  - sick trust writes what clients need into a directory: the root as PEM,
    DER and Apple configuration profile, the intermediates and further
    roots, ca_bundle.pem, a page with the fingerprints and the installation
    steps for each platform, and the scripts trust-linux.sh, trust-macos.sh,
    trust-windows.ps1 and trust-nss.sh. The scripts install only when given
    the SHA-256 fingerprint of every root from another channel and remove
    exactly what they installed. System stores and Android get the root
    only; an intermediate goes where it is kept without trust, or into
    ca_bundle.pem as a program's own CA file. The page says which programs
    keep their own store and marks what was tested in containers.
  - Docker (docker/): a Compose setup of step-ca, SICK on a schedule and a
    web server for the trust page. Lab mode makes a CA at the first start;
    import mode builds it on a hierarchy made offline by ceremony.sh (root
    and name-constrained intermediate, with a ceremony record, also for
    email addresses and URIs; --renew-intermediate for the next one).
    init-ca.sh declares further provisioners in a JSON file, limits the
    ACME challenge types, sets the lifetimes of the provisioner "acme"
    (serverAuth only, 30 days) and takes email and URI entries in the CA
    policy; --switch-intermediate and --retire-intermediate switch a CA with
    an overlap, --rotate-provisioner replaces key and password of "sick".
    ca-backup.sh backs up the CA volume with a manifest and restores it into
    an empty volume only. The image carries SICK-Target-Proxmox; the build
    context needs three checkouts. Container tests are in t/e2e/.
  - Kubernetes (k8s/): sick run as a CronJob with metrics and alert rules for
    failed Jobs, and cert-manager getting certificates from the lab CA by
    ACME and by step-issuer.
  - contrib/: systemd units and a cron line that run sick, write the
    Prometheus textfile and the trust page and notify on failure
    (sick-notify, by webhook or mail); Prometheus alert rules relative to
    each device's renew_before, tested with promtool; ACME recipes for
    Traefik, Caddy and nginx against the lab CA; device recipes for Linux
    hosts over SSH, MikroTik RouterOS, Proxmox VE and Backup Server, and an
    nginx reading its certificate from SICK's state volume; an mTLS recipe
    with client certificates from the provisioner "clients".
  - The handbook (docs/handbook/): planning, the CA, clients, services,
    devices, operations, emergencies and client certificates, with test
    reports, a checklist of what is left to try, and the decisions that are
    the maintainer's.
